==French==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/vwa.la/|lang=fr}}

===Etymology===
From {{term|vois||see!, look!|lang=fr}}, second-person singular imperative of {{term|voir||to see, to look|lang=fr}} and {{term|là||there|lang=fr}}, literally meaning "look there!".

===Verb===
{{fr-verb}}

# [[there|There]] is.
#* Beckett, Samuel
#*: '''''Voilà''' l’homme tout entier, s’en prenant à sa chaussure alors que c’est son pied le coupable.''
#*:: '''''There is''' man in his entirety, blaming his shoe when his foot is guilty.''
# [[here|Here]] is.
#: '''''Voilà''' le fromage''
#:: '''''Here's''' the cheese''
# [[that|That]] is.
#: '''''Voilà''' ce que je lui ai demandé, et voici sa réponse : « ... »''
#:: '''''That's''' what I asked her and this is her answer: "..."''

====Usage notes====
* ''voilà'' is a [[defective]] verb. Its only conjugation is in the present indicative tense, even though it can appear in phrases that imply another tense.
* It is mainly used to introduce a slightly distant person or object, in contrast to {{term|voici|lang=fr}} which is used to designate a person or object near the speaker.
* In face-to-face conversations where both participants can see the subject of the conversation, ''voilà'' often supersedes {{term|voici|lang=fr}} (thus its additional definition: ''Here is'').

